1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:10,000
You type a question and get an answer in your tone. Nobody typed that answer in. Five stages did.

2
00:00:10,000 --> 00:00:20,000
Picture a student who read the whole library before learning any manners. That is a chat model. Reading is first. Manners come later.

3
00:00:20,000 --> 00:00:30,500
Stage one. Text is collected, cleaned, and cut into tokens, small word pieces. The post argues quality matters more than sheer volume.

4
00:00:30,500 --> 00:00:41,500
Stage two is pretraining. The only job is guessing the next token, over and over, across trillions of tokens.

5
00:00:41,500 --> 00:00:51,500
What comes out is a base model. Brilliant autocomplete. Ask it a question and it may just continue your sentence.

6
00:00:51,500 --> 00:01:02,500
Stage three. Supervised fine-tuning. Thousands of worked examples of prompts and good responses teach it the shape of being helpful.

7
00:01:02,500 --> 00:01:13,500
Stage four. People rank several answers. A separate model, the reward model, learns to predict those rankings. Now it can score millions.

8
00:01:13,500 --> 00:01:25,000
Stage five. Generate, score with the reward model, update, repeat. This loop is what people mean by reinforcement learning from human feedback.

9
00:01:25,000 --> 00:01:36,000
It was optimized to produce likely text, not true text. When it lacks the facts, it still writes fluently. Fluency is not evidence.

10
00:01:36,000 --> 00:01:44,000
The post's recurring lesson: better data beats more data at every stage.

11
00:01:44,000 --> 00:01:54,000
Tokens. Pretraining. Fine-tuning. A reward model. Practice. Five stages, one pipeline.

12
00:01:54,000 --> 00:02:02,500
The realistic next step is building a tiny stage-two model yourself. The article links where to start.
